          "guid": "ms5tdhu86gnt3kd5",
          "message": {
            "text": "The RENDERERS table is the point of this file: the expectations run against every ticket renderer, so a renderer that forgets an indicator fails instead of going visually unnoticed.\n\nThe only coverage before HS-9487 was the pure isTicketBlocked predicate (ticketRowHelpers.test.ts (0)). That is structurally blind to this bug class — a predicate test cannot notice that a renderer never calls the predicate, which is exactly how createColumnCard shipped without the border and stayed that way.\n\nTwo consequences worth preserving:\n\n- The tests drive the real factories (createTicketRow / createColumnCard / both preview variants) rather than a hand-built DOM. A hand-built row asserts the shape the test author believed in, not the shape the JSX literal emits — and the missing class lived in the JSX literal.\n- Adding a renderer is one row in the table; adding an indicator is one it() that then covers all four. The cost of keeping this honest is deliberately near zero.\n\nThe EFFECT_TARGETS table does the same for the reactive half, which matters because bindList preserves a row across data changes — create-time correctness alone would leave the border stale for the entire life of the row.\n\nSee also e2e/blocked-indicator.spec.ts, which covers what a class-name assertion cannot: whether any CSS rule actually matches.\n",
            "markdown": "The `RENDERERS` table is the point of this file: the expectations run against every ticket renderer, so a renderer that forgets an indicator fails instead of going visually unnoticed.\n\nThe only coverage before HS-9487 was the pure `isTicketBlocked` predicate ([ticketRowHelpers.test.ts](0)). That is structurally blind to this bug class — a predicate test cannot notice that a renderer never *calls* the predicate, which is exactly how `createColumnCard` shipped without the border and stayed that way.\n\nTwo consequences worth preserving:\n\n- The tests drive the **real factories** (`createTicketRow` / `createColumnCard` / both preview variants) rather than a hand-built DOM. A hand-built row asserts the shape the test author believed in, not the shape the JSX literal emits — and the missing class lived in the JSX literal.\n- Adding a renderer is one row in the table; adding an indicator is one `it()` that then covers all four. The cost of keeping this honest is deliberately near zero.\n\nThe `EFFECT_TARGETS` table does the same for the reactive half, which matters because `bindList` preserves a row across data changes — create-time correctness alone would leave the border stale for the entire life of the row.\n\nSee also `e2e/blocked-indicator.spec.ts`, which covers what a class-name assertion cannot: whether any CSS rule actually matches.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "src/client/ticketBlockedIndicator.test.tsx"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 62,
                  "endLine": 75,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "    { id: 'n2', text: 'FEEDBACK NEEDED: which option should I build?', created_at: '2026-01-02T00:00:00Z' },\n  ]);\n}\n\n/**\n * Every renderer that produces a user-visible ticket element. The indicator\n * expectations below run against all of them — ADD A ROW HERE when you add a\n * renderer, and the whole suite covers it.\n */\nconst RENDERERS: { name: string; render: (t: Ticket) => HTMLElement }[] = [\n  { name: 'createTicketRow (list view)', render: createTicketRow },\n  { name: 'createColumnCard (column view)', render: createColumnCard },\n  { name: 'createPreviewRow (list preview)', render: createPreviewRow },\n  { name: 'createPreviewColumnCard (column preview)', render: createPreviewColumnCard },"
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
